      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Given that two of the roots of the equation \\(ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d = 0\\) are \\(3\\) and \\(-2\\), we can express the polynomial in its factored form:\n\n\\[\na(x - 3)(x + 2)\n\\]\n\nLet's expand this expression:\n\n\\[\na(x - 3)(x + 2) = a[(x - 3)(x + 2)] = a[x^2 - 3x + 2x - 6] = a[x^2 - x - 6]\n\\]\n\nThus, the expanded form of the polynomial is:\n\n\\[\nax^3 - ax^2 - 6a\n\\]\n\nFrom this, we can identify the coefficients:\n- The coefficient of \\(x^2\\) is \\(-a\\).\n- The coefficient of \\(x\\) is \\(-a\\).\n- The constant term is \\(-6a\\).\n\nAccording to Vieta's formulas, the sum of the roots of a polynomial \\(ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d = 0\\) is given by \\(-\\frac{b}{a}\\). Here, the roots are \\(3\\), \\(-2\\), and the third root \\(r\\). The sum of the roots is:\n\n\\[\n3 + (-2) + r = 1 + r\n\\]\n\nBy Vieta's formulas, we have:\n\n\\[\n-\\frac{b}{a} = 1 + r\n\\]\n\nWe need to determine \\(\\frac{b+c}{a}\\). First, we find \\(b\\) and \\(c\\):\n- The coefficient of \\(x\\) (which is \\(c\\)) is \\(-a\\).\n- The constant term (which is \\(d\\)) can be found by expanding the polynomial completely or using Vieta's formulas directly.\n\nTo proceed, we use the information about the constant term. The constant term \\(d\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\nd = -6a\n\\]\n\nSince we already have all the coefficients, we can now compute \\(\\frac{b+c}{a}\\):\n\n\\[\nb = -a, \\quad c = -a\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\nb + c = -a + (-a) = -2a\n\\]\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b+c}{a} = \\frac{-2a}{a} = -2\n\\]\n\nThe answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-2}\n\\]",
