      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Given that two of the roots of the cubic equation \\( ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d = 0 \\) are 3 and -2, we know from Vieta's formulas that the sum of the roots taken one at a time is given by \\(-\\frac{b}{a}\\). Therefore:\n\n\\[\n3 + (-2) + r = -\\frac{b}{a}\n\\]\n\nHere, \\( r \\) is the third root. Solving for \\( r \\):\n\n\\[\n3 - 2 + r = -\\frac{b}{a} \\implies 1 + r = -\\frac{b}{a} \\implies r = -\\frac{b}{a} - 1\n\\]\n\nNext, Vieta's formula for the product of the roots taken two at a time gives us:\n\n\\[\n3 \\cdot (-2) + 3 \\cdot r + (-2) \\cdot r = \\frac{c}{a}\n\\]\n\nCalculating the product terms and simplifying:\n\n\\[\n3(-2) + 3r - 2r = \\frac{c}{a} \\implies -6 + r = \\frac{c}{a} \\implies r = \\frac{c}{a} + 6\n\\]\n\nSince we have two different expressions for \\( r \\):\n\n\\[\nr = -\\frac{b}{a} - 1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad r = \\frac{c}{a} + 6\n\\]\n\nSetting these equal to each other:\n\n\\[\n-\\frac{b}{a} - 1 = \\frac{c}{a} + 6\n\\]\n\nTo eliminate the fractions, multiply through by \\( a \\):\n\n\\[\n-b - a = c + 6a\n\\]\n\nRearrange to isolate terms involving \\( b \\) and \\( c \\):\n\n\\[\n-b - c = a + 6a \\implies -b - c = 7a \\implies c + b = -7a\n\\]\n\nWe need to compute \\(\\frac{b + c}{a}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b + c}{a} = \\frac{-7a}{a} = -7\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-7}\n\\]",
